title: "pallet-revive: measure the contract's own account in host function benchmarks"
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `CallSetup` no longer whitelists the executing contract's `System::Account`, so its accesses
    are now included in the weights of `seal_call_transfer`, `seal_instantiate`,
    `seal_instantiate_transfer`, `evm_instantiate`, `evm_instantiate_transfer`, `seal_terminate`
    and `seal_terminate_logic`. Runtimes on the pallet's default weights need no action, and the
    `WeightInfo` trait is unchanged. Runtimes with their own `pallet_revive` weights
    have to re-run the benchmarks. So do precompile benchmarks built with
    `pallet_revive::call_builder::CallSetup` or its re-export
    `pallet_revive::precompiles::run::CallSetup` that access the executing contract's account.
    A benchmark that relied on the previous behavior of `CallSetup::new` or `CallSetup::default`
    now has to whitelist the account explicitly, and only where another weight already pays for
    the account (`seal_balance` is the one exception, until #12673). For example:
    ```rust
    let account_id = &call_setup.contract().account_id;
    frame_benchmarking::whitelist_account!(account_id);
    ```
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    - A contract that sends value or creates a contract, `SELFDESTRUCT`, the PolkaVM `terminate`
      syscall and the System precompile's `terminate` now also pay for the contract's own account:
      one read, a write where the balance or the nonce changes, and its proof size. Previously,
      their weights left the account out. Terminating a contract pays for reading the account
      twice, in `seal_terminate` and `seal_terminate_logic`, and for writing it once, in
      `seal_terminate_logic`. The `call` and `instantiate` extrinsics are unchanged.
    - Reading the contract's own balance with `BALANCE` or the `balance_of` syscall now does the
      same work and costs the same as with `SELFBALANCE` or the `balance` syscall, so a
      `transfer` or `send` receiver can afford it. The weight of these reads excludes the
      contract's own account, so a nested contract called without value reads it without paying,
      until #12673.
    - In the prestate tracer's diff mode, a contract whose constructor reads its own balance with
      `BALANCE` and then reverts no longer appears in the post state, as in geth.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
